notes.body:
The biggest single thing to improve tapDraw is to pre-calculate the group keys at least for GroupBySeverity into QStringLiterals, the default (and what is used at initial tapDraw time, while the dialog controls are disabled) at compile time. C++23 has constexpr to_chars for integer values, but even without that any sort of variation on:

```c++
#define PI_STRLITERAL(severity) QStringLiteral(#severity)
...
    switch (grouping_mode_) {
    case GroupBySeverity:
        switch (record.severity) {
        case PI_ERROR:
            return PI_STRLITERAL(PI_ERROR);
        case PI_WARN:    
            return PI_STRLITERAL(PI_WARN);
        case PI_NOTE:    
            return PI_STRLITERAL(PI_NOTE);
        case PI_CHAT:    
            return PI_STRLITERAL(PI_CHAT);
        case PI_COMMENT: 
            return PI_STRLITERAL(PI_COMMENT);
        default:
            return QString::number(record.severity);
        }
```

is fairly easy. (If the string should be a stringified version of the actual hex value, then:
```
#define PI_STR(severity) #severity
#define PI_STRLITERAL(severity) QStringLiteral(PI_STR(severity))
```
etc., or just write literals into the program.)

That by itself takes the number of instructions for the 200k Release case down 10% of the overall total. tapDraw by itself goes from 1655136865 to 619280889 decreasing by more than half.

